#{[17592186088739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1061 40237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "758bd934-4f53-5080-bca6-e617305ccced" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder.\n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1$.\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1065 6639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee084b63-b5c9-54fe-8f73-750445535efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1064 "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**.\n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2$, and $4$.\n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$\n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1065 6640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db98b259-0f36-53b5-b62a-788af51d2b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1067 "Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1064 "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both.\n\nThe only exception is the number $1$. The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1065 6646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2092df40-d7b5-5553-a2d1-05d8166f06bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1067 "One Is Neither Prime Nor Composite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Segments and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d9e4943a-72d7-545e-b746-3a19ee6d3a83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Rays and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f50316d-ff00-53a1-af3a-fedd68e91f43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Lines and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8db8b0ae-1a2a-546a-884d-bdf773c75483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1045 "Points, Lines, Rays, and Segments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106005 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bd3c7493-58ee-57a8-8d3a-3cd07eb7026e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106833 #edb/ref 1061 40003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106833 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a8029d6-f569-5e02-94f4-a8c43054eba8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106833 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106834 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106834 #edb/ref 1061 40094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106834 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79e005c1-44a4-5a62-80df-af13329c4744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106834 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106835 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106835 #edb/ref 1061 40242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106835 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0bee6a5d-8341-54c8-b8a6-8740368cff99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106835 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106836 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106836 #edb/ref 1061 40243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106836 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f3e9956-cbc9-58f4-8ef4-625ad217137b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106836 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106957 #edb/ref 1061 8689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106957 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5170c6de-909a-58ce-863d-4886baeefb53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106957 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106958 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106958 #edb/ref 1061 8690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106958 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ecf8f9e-5978-5c97-8a02-ed6688aea1aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106958 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107041 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll discuss some fundamental objects in geometry. The objects we'll discuss are some of the building blocks we use to describe all geometrical objects.\n\nA **point** is an exact location in space. It has no size (i.e., no length, width, or depth), only position. A point is indicated with a dot usually labeled with a capital letter (e.g., $P$, $Q$, $S$,...), as shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/aceb9052aee93f2d7ef5cfc3f6a9c7ace291e6c9525751a2d826c0fbfa0b36ed.png)\n\nA **line** is a straight object that is infinitely long and has no width. We can think of it as an infinite collection of points, extending infinitely in both directions. A line has no endpoints!\n\nLet's take a look at the following line.\n\n![](images/86/861fbcc84e4e293379616a5e18d9073ecfdf4af5f6d17dece22196d332b62899.png)\n\nA line that passes through the points $P$ and $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longleftrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **both** directions.\n\nLines can also be indicated with lowercase letters. For example:\n\n![](images/0a/0a2d566ad6126527f4b1a2b5a992688a668b9ff901c6b7716381521a576a0594.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107041 #edb/ref 1065 2161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107041 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6361854-d380-59ab-8f5c-a64142f383c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107041 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107135 #edb/ref 1064 "A **ray** is similar to a line but has a starting point and extends infinitely in one direction only. An example of a ray is shown below.\n\n![](images/75/750aef546281b34fef8e9aef61c1260894128138022e971d6c19dfd834cfa1e2.png)\n\nA ray that starts at the point $P$ and passes through a point $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **one** direction. Notice that the arrow's tail is above the starting point $P$.\n\nSimilar to lines, rays have no width but are infinite in length." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107135 #edb/ref 1065 6647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107135 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74865d26-a257-5d2d-a7cd-537189af9954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107135 #edb/ref 1067 "Rays" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107136 #edb/ref 1064 "A **segment** is part of a line bounded by two points, called the **endpoints** of the segment. An example of a segment is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0d8c12318cc57412be73f2cfb077efa5b06472554e75be41af233c87a9ab17.png)\n\nA segment with endpoints $A$ and $B$ is denoted $\\overline{AB}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107136 #edb/ref 1065 6648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107136 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee97c2fb-0c77-5af6-b979-587c04a1f0c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107136 #edb/ref 1067 "Segments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Cube of a Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133738 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "39ed44f2-c5ca-5920-9bb0-4ea26ff3207a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Cube of a Negative Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133739 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4cb81a65-ae88-591a-bb87-0ecb7124b154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Cube of a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133740 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "70e7585e-7380-5dbc-9272-b358f085429c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Cube of a Decimal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133741 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "09de45b3-1a35-55e3-9886-2e0533f237d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1045 "Cubing Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134039 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "682c47c0-744b-5245-9512-733920b3dc7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134596 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134596 #edb/ref 1061 151851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ccabb01-ff56-5686-b294-bd66b4e89e88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134597 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134597 #edb/ref 1061 151852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134597 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45f96aa2-301d-5486-9223-105546674dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1061 151856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd815b69-da3f-5d4e-b49a-e75f1d8f0398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1061 151857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79b94128-fada-50aa-8ec5-3f3315b85c77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134599 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1061 151861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "266a9ffe-5ee6-5f21-95a7-a8aea3b7a60d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1061 151864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fd6a3e8-b2b4-5760-a3c1-067f79a80a5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1064 "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4$, and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3$, so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2$:\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8$.\n\nClearly,\n\n$$\n1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nThe next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n$1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1065 8272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1bd95aec-179b-5dc4-b7bd-da02ca5932f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135183 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1064 "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(-)\\cdot(-) = (-)\\cdot(-)_(⏟)_((+))\\cdot(-) = (+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1065 8273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e462ead2-4e55-5dac-a940-22768a77546e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135184 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cube of a Negative Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138397 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138397 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Repeated Multiplication of One-Digit Numbers Using Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138397 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138397 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4b74abc9-9563-5e6e-bdae-3371ff742ba9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138398 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138398 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Repeated Multiplication of Larger Numbers Using Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138398 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138398 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "59d063a5-e148-544b-a42a-7d8a1ee10c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138399 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138399 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Exponents as Repeated Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138399 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138399 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f80c4a5c-c03a-5b5a-afab-d39abc720598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing Larger Exponents as Repeated Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138400 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a49001aa-bf42-5005-9bf0-940caab49b8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138543 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138543 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138543 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Absolute Value of a Positive Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138543 #edb/ref 10