#edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86003208-694a-59f7-a78d-1d9cef22ac2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143730 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143730 #edb/ref 1061 174398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143730 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00476cba-2f5d-5364-9604-f4ca1d6c706b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143730 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143731 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143731 #edb/ref 1061 174401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143731 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826383f3-7e5a-57f1-a9a9-8d7e2104cdbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143731 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186144075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143732 #edb/ref 1061 174402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9043ac43-54a0-55ac-af0b-b8209415b37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143733 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186143734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143733 #edb/ref 1061 174405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc73d26f-4867-5213-9687-4bb629751e8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186144076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143734 #edb/ref 1061 174406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a54e63c9-f396-57e3-973b-4cb92a8cc901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186143734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186142840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144029 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. But can we do this without using models?\n\nAs it turns out, models give us vital clues on how this should be done!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's take a look at the fraction model for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 9 \\times 3\n$$\n\n![](images/47/4700c7ddbdc849a73d69ba41d2d716218f1fb181e8e5e9b8757392db6d9a6243.png)\n\nIn the answer, we end up with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}\\times {\\color{red}{3}} = 6\n$$\n\nparts, and the denominator remains the same. This is always the case.\n\nTherefore, to multiply a fraction by a whole number, we follow the following steps:\n\n> **Step 1**: We multiply the numerator by the whole number.\n> **Step 2**: We keep the denominator the same.\n\nUsing these steps, we can figure out the answer without using models:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{9}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}\\,\\times 3\\,}}{9} = \\dfrac{6}{9}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144029 #edb/ref 1065 8393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1ad38dc-1875-52f8-ad8b-fb013451e541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144037 #edb/ref 1064 "The **least common multiple** of two numbers is the smallest value that is a multiple of both the numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$.\n\nWe start by listing the multiples of each number. Then we find the smallest number that appears in both lists.\n\n- Multiples of $3$: $\\quad 3, 6, 9, 12, {\\color{blue}15}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $5$: $\\quad 5, 10, {\\color{blue}15}, 20, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$ is ${\\color{blue}{15}}$.\n\nWe can visualize the least common multiple of $3$ and $5$ using a diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/67/67254f47d80d6c319166affbf77c083b3b9e1c8c9ce79d9ee6016772c139ad69.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144037 #edb/ref 1065 8423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b006bb6-a2b9-5b46-bb2d-9cc360ae0ca4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144074 #edb/ref 1064 "The **reciprocal** of a fraction is a bit like turning it upside down: we switch the numerator and denominator.\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nwe switch the numerator and the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}20} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20} 1 = 20\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nis $20$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144074 #edb/ref 1065 8576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144074 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db280674-4a34-5dbd-99a3-c1c2c0ecf8be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144074 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144075 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the reciprocal of a whole number, we must first write the number as a fraction.\n\nFor instance, let's write the number $2$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n2= \\dfrac{2} 1\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144075 #edb/ref 1065 8577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144075 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "439c60e5-dcf3-50b0-bc8f-a2d2ce74cdcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144075 #edb/ref 1067 "The Reciprocal of a Whole Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144076 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the reciprocal of a mixed number, we must first convert it to an improper fraction.\n\nLet's find the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}3} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144076 #edb/ref 1065 8578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144076 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae66510a-0eed-5ee9-8a6e-20d52291d930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186144076 #edb/ref 1067 "The Reciprocal of a Mixed Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147404 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147405 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147405 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147405 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
